# ============================================================
#  Baseline pools: the castle towers (phase 3, step 1b).
#  Repeatable routine events for the observatory, Mir's workshop,
#  the Ledger office, the headmistress's tower, the Restricted
#  Section, the third gargoyle and the seventh tower, plus the
#  hidden-course hints for artifice, starcalling and hollow studies.
#  No clues, no arc moves: these places stay mysterious.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The Observatory ----------------

@event observatory_night_log
time: evening night
title: The Night Log
place: observatory
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study magic
scene: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.
text: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|{flag(wrote_night_log): Since your last entry|Since you were last here}, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes "cloudy".} {season == "winter": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}
> left: Log what the telescope finds
  flag wrote_night_log
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}
> right: Read the old entries instead
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {A winter entry from long ago says "Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it."|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}

@event observatory_one_star_each
time: evening night
title: One Star Each
place: observatory
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: star
tags: social magic
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)
scene: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.
text: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added "two if they're small" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == "summer": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}
> left: Share the eyepiece
  rel here +small
  health -tiny
  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {"That one," Seraphine says, "is going to matter in, oh." She doesn't finish.|"Left of it," Seraphine says. "Next spring's left." You look anyway.|"There," Seraphine says, and moves your hand an inch. "That's the one you'll want. Don't ask me when."}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.|Kael shares the eyepiece without being asked. Below, the bridges go out one by one, and neither of you counts them.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}
> right: Wait your turn properly
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. "You'll want it," she says. "Later. Now, I mean."|"Yours is the next one," Seraphine says, and it is.|Seraphine steps back as you arrive, as if she'd been holding your place. "Right on time," she says, puzzling you.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.|Kael gives up the eyepiece the moment you arrive and stays to watch the bridges go out with you.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}

@event observatory_roof_ledge
title: The Roof Ledge
place: observatory
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: moon
tags: life danger
scene: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.
text: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == "winter": The tiles are white with frost.|{The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}
> left: Climb out onto the ledge
  challenge feat apprentice:
    win:
      mood +small
      lore +tiny
      say: {You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.|You step over the loose tile without looking. Someone's scarf is still on the gutter; you leave it, for whoever comes next.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.|You reach for the snagged scarf and the gutter reaches for you. You get back in with the scarf and a bruise.}
> right: Watch through the glass
  lore +tiny
  say: {You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.|You watch through the glass. On the ledge outside, someone else is sitting where you'd have sat, and they wave.}

@event observatory_scraped_word
time: evening night
title: The Scraped-Out Word
place: observatory
when: not enrolled(starcalling) and not passed(starcalling, 1)
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 8
weight: 6
art: star
tags: study mystery
scene: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.
text: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {In one margin, "Ask. Be told."|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}
> left: Trace the gaps by lamplight
  find heard_of_starcalling
  lore +small
  mana -small
  say: You feed a thread of witchlight into the gaps, and it drinks more than you meant to give. {The words around the gaps say "called", "named", "down". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|"...and the light came down when I said its name..." Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.|The shape around the gap is a verb. Whatever it was, it was done to a star, and it was taught, not written.}
> right: Ask the telescope, half joking
  lore +tiny
  say: {The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.|The telescope turns, very slowly, to the same star as always. You're beginning to think it's a reminder, not an answer.}

# ---------------- Mir's Workshop ----------------

@event workshop_spare_bench
title: The Spare Bench
place: workshop
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: gear
tags: magic life
scene: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.
text: {Mir keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mir has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {Mir waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == "winter": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}
> left: Mend a jammed music box
  alchemy +small
  mood -tiny
  health -tiny
  say: A loose spring bites your thumb on the way in. {It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mir says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}
> right: Just watch Mir work
  mood +small
  rel mir +tiny
  say: {Mir explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mir hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}

@event workshop_volunteer
title: A Volunteer With Hands
place: workshop
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: gear
tags: social magic
cast:
  maker: mir
scene: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.
text: {"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!" {maker} holds up {a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}
> left: Try it on
  chance likely:
    alchemy +small
    rel maker +tiny
    mood +tiny
    say: {It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.|It works. You still don't know what it was for, and {maker} is too delighted to tell you.}
  else:
    health -small
    rel maker +small
    say: {It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying "Interesting!" in a voice that means sorry.|It goes off with a pop and a smell of hot brass. {maker} writes "promising" and hands you the bucket.}
> right: Hold the fire bucket
  rel maker +tiny
  say: {You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.|You hold the bucket. You need it twice, which {maker} calls an improvement.}

@event workshop_stirring_cauldron
title: The Self-Stirring Cauldron
place: workshop
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cauldron
tags: magic danger
scene: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.
text: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == "spring": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}
> left: Adjust the enchantment
  challenge enchant apprentice+:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mir, passing, says "Oh!" and then "Huh," and then "Teach me."|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.|You adjust it, gently, the way it seems to want. It stirs the soup for nobody a little more cheerfully.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.|It recognises you, and holds a grudge. The spoon flicks soup at you until you back away.}
> right: Take notes from a safe distance
  lore +tiny
  say: {You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.|You take notes from the doorway. The spoon pauses, as if waiting for you to finish the sentence.}

@event workshop_glass_cabinet
title: The Glass Cabinet
place: workshop
when: not enrolled(artifice) and not passed(artifice, 1)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 8
weight: 6
art: gear
tags: study magic
scene: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.
text: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mir's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mir says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: "Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II."
> left: Study the machines through the glass
  alchemy +small
  say: {Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.|The machines don't move, but you're sure they're thinking. One of them, very slightly, nods.}
> right: Try the cabinet lock
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive journeyman-:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.|The lock opens at a touch, as if it had been waiting. The machines look at you, and then, satisfied, at each other.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      renown -tiny
      say: {The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mir is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mir says it does that, probably, to people it likes.|The lock doesn't bite this time. It simply refuses, the way a cat refuses, and Mir says that's progress.}

# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger Office ----------------

@event ledger_office_unprintable
title: Letters We Will Not Print
place: ledger_office
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: life
scene: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.
text: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == "winter": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}
> left: Read the whole basket
  mood +tiny
  say: {The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}
> right: Write one of your own
  flag ledger_letter_sent
  chance unlikely:
    renown +small
    mood +tiny
    say: {They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.|They print it, with a footnote from the editor disagreeing. It's the most-read thing in the issue.}
  else:
    mood -tiny
    say: {It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: "Too sensible." You aren't sure that's a criticism.|It goes in the basket. You find it there next week, with someone else's reply pinned to it.}

@event ledger_office_deadline_night
title: Deadline Night
place: ledger_office
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: social
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)
scene: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.
text: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}
> left: Fold until the press stops
  flag ledger_folded
  rel here +small
  health -small
  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {"Good. Fast. Again," says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.|Nessa doesn't say anything. She just slides the next stack to you, which is the highest honour on deadline night.}|{By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.|You fold in rhythm with the press. When it stops, you keep going for a moment, out of habit.}}
> right: Make your excuses
  mood +small
  rel here -small
  renown -tiny
  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {"Deserter," Nessa calls. "Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday."|Nessa doesn't look up. "Coward," she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.|Nessa looks up for once. "Fine. Go. You owe me a Thursday." You do, and she collects.}|{You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.|You leave, and the press shudders to a stop as you go. Somebody blames you, and it goes in the paper.}}

@event ledger_office_drying_line
title: The Drying Line
place: ledger_office
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: life
scene: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.
text: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {One item names your house.|One item begins "Which second-year was seen..."|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}
> left: Read ahead
  challenge deceive apprentice+:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +small
      say: {You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.|You read the item under the red peg. It's about you, and it's true, and it's very short. You leave it exactly as it was.}
    lose:
      renown -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {Caught. The next column has an item beginning "Which nosy visitor..."|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.|You lift the red peg and the whole line comes down. Tomorrow's column has a new item, and it's about you.}
> right: Leave the line alone
  mood -tiny
  say: {You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.|You leave it. You know which peg it is, and that's almost the same as knowing.}

# ---------------- The Headmistress's Tower ----------------

@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl
time: evening
title: The Stone Owl
place: headmistress_tower
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: owl
tags: magic
scene: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.
text: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. "A question first," it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == "winter": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}
> left: Answer the question
  challenge research journeyman wild:
    win:
      lore +small
      rel isolde +tiny
      say: {The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|"Adequate," says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.|You answer before it finishes asking. The owl looks at you with both eyes for a long time, then closes them.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {"No," says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.|Your answer is right and the owl says "No" anyway, to see what you'll do. You climb on. It seems satisfied.}
> right: Climb past without a word
  guile +small
  rel isolde -tiny
  say: {The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.|You climb past and nod to it, as you'd nod to a porter. It nods back, very slightly.}

@event headmistress_tower_tea
title: Two Cups Poured
place: headmistress_tower
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: social
cast:
  host: isolde
scene: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.
text: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. "Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going."|Two cups, already poured. "Well," says {host}, "how is it really going?"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: "Your last year," she says. "I remember your first."|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}
> left: Tell her the truth
  rel host +small
  mood -small
  say: {She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, "Yes. I thought so." Somehow that helps, later.|You tell her before she asks. She hears it all, then tells you something true about her own first years. It doesn't help, and then it does.}
> right: Give her the polished version
  challenge deceive vs isolde.guile -2:
    win:
      charm +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|"Very good," she says. "Next time, the true one." She means both halves.|She lets the polished version stand and pours more tea. "You've got better at it," she says. You can't tell if that's praise.}
    lose:
      rel host -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {"Mm," she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.|She hears the polished version, then repeats the one sentence of it that was true. You talk for an hour after that.}

@event headmistress_tower_window_seat
title: The Window Seat
place: headmistress_tower
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study
scene: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.
text: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. "Read anything. Break nothing."|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == "autumn": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}
> left: Read what she left out
  lore +small
  say: {A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says "No."|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}
> right: Doze on the window seat
  mood +small
  health +tiny
  rel isolde -tiny
  say: {You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through most of the visit. When you wake, she is still writing, and gives you a look over her spectacles that says she had hoped you would read.|You doze in your window seat. You wake to find she's moved her desk lamp so it doesn't shine in your eyes.}

# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------

@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves
title: The Breathing Shelves
place: restricted_section
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study mystery
scene: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.
text: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == "winter": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}
> left: Read until the candle gutters
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.|You read in your chair by a low blue candle. The shelves breathe with you, and the chapter goes in like a held breath.}
> right: Read only the spines
  lore +tiny
  mana -tiny
  say: {Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.|You walk the spines, and they make room for you as you pass. One spine you haven't seen before has no title at all.}

@event restricted_section_lantern
title: Holding the Lantern
place: restricted_section
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: social mystery
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)
scene: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.
text: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {"Did that book just sigh?" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: "I'll miss this," {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}
> left: Read while they keep watch
  lore +small
  rel friend -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.|You read; {friend} holds the light where you point, and the shadows walk where {they} hold{s} it. Neither of you says much on the way back.}
> right: Take the lantern for a while
  rel friend +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.|You take the lantern and {friend} reads to you, a page you'll both remember. The shadows stop walking to listen.}

@event restricted_section_chained_book
time: evening
title: The Chained Book
place: restricted_section
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: chain
tags: mystery danger
scene: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.
text: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}
> left: Read the open page
  challenge research adept-:
    win:
      lore +small
      mana +small
      say: A thread of stored power runs up your arm from the page. {The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.|The page stays blank while you look and fills when you blink. You read it in blinks. It takes all night.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.|You look away and the page fills; you look back and it's blank, and smug. Your eyes ache for a day.}
> right: Close it and step back
  mood -tiny
  mana +tiny
  say: As the cover shuts, something that had been quietly drawing on your magic lets go. {The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.|You close it before it can finish expecting you. The chain lies still, a link shorter.}

@event restricted_section_handleless_door
time: evening
title: The Door Without a Handle
place: restricted_section
when: not enrolled(hollow_studies) and not passed(hollow_studies, 1)
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 8
weight: 6
art: door
tags: study mystery
scene: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.
text: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: "Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why."
> left: Knock
  rel isolde +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, "Not yet."|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it. Next day the headmistress nods to you in the corridor, as if she heard.|You knock, as you always do. This time, from far away, someone knocks back, once, as if to say they've noted it.}
> right: Copy the chalk into your notes
  lore +small
  mana -small
  say: Copying the chalk draws on your magic like a cold draught. {The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.|You copy it from memory and check it against the door. The chalk agrees with you, which feels like being agreed with.}

# ---------------- The Third Gargoyle ----------------

@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks
title: Marks on the Wall
place: gargoyle_passage
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: map
tags: life mystery
scene: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.
text: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == "winter": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}
> left: Add your own mark
  renown +tiny
  guile +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}|You add your mark beside the initials that keep coming back. Next time, there's a tiny arrow pointing to yours.}
  flag left_wall_mark
> right: Follow an old arrow
  lore +small
  say: {The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.|You follow the arrow you know lies, to see how it lies. It lies beautifully, all the way to a view of the stars.}

@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish
time: evening
title: The Gargoyle's Dish
place: gargoyle_passage
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: coin
tags: magic life
scene: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.
text: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == "summer": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}
> left: Leave a few crowns in the dish
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {By morning the crowns are gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crowns clink in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.|You pay the gargoyle its crowns, like rent. The night is calm, and the stair doesn't move once on your way back.}
> right: Borrow the biscuit
  alignment -tiny
  chance even:
    mood +small
    health +tiny
    say: {It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.|You borrow the peeled grape. The gargoyle's look follows you down the stair, but the grape is excellent.}
  else:
    mood -small
    say: {It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.|You take the grape, and the gargoyle collects. Your boots are full of rainwater by the bottom of the stair.}

@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards
title: Cards on the Parapet
place: gargoyle_passage
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cards
tags: social
cast:
  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)
scene: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.
text: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons, and crowns for anyone who dares.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: "One more for the road," {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}
> left: Play a hand
  when: gold >= 8
  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:
    win:
      gold +tiny
      mood +small
      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {"Well, look at you," Corvin says, paying out. "Who taught you that? Don't say me."|"Remind me never to owe you money," Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons and, grudgingly, crowns.|Corvin deals you in, loses, and laughs. "You're getting good. I'll have to start cheating properly."}|{You win the pot, buttons and a few actual crowns.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons, and the crowns under them, with dignity.|You're dealt in before you sit and win before you've warmed up. The pot is mostly buttons, and some of it is crowns.}}
    lose:
      gold -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {Corvin scoops the pot and winces. "Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing." He gives you your buttons back, at least.|"Never bluff a man who owes three people money," Corvin says, kindly.|Corvin deals from the bottom and you know it and can't prove it. You lose with a smile, which annoys him.}|{You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.|You lose the hand to a card the pigeon was standing on. The pigeon will not be questioned.}}
> right: Watch the lake instead
  mood +tiny
  health -tiny
  rel here +tiny
  say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {"Saint," Corvin says, not looking up, "you'll never be rich." He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.|Corvin deals you out without asking and hands you his scarf before the wind does. "Lake's nice tonight," he says. It is.}|{You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.|You watch the lake hold the lantern still. Behind you, someone wins, and someone else blames the pigeon.}}

# ---------------- The Seventh Tower ----------------

@event seventh_tower_stair_count
title: The Stair That Changes
place: seventh_tower
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: tower
tags: mystery
scene: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.
text: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == "winter": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}
> left: Count every step
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}
  flag counted_seventh_stair
> right: Climb without counting
  mana +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.|You climb without counting, candle leaning ahead. You're at the top before you've thought about it, and it feels allowed.}

@event seventh_tower_dust_room
time: evening
title: Dust and Moonlight
place: seventh_tower
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: moon
tags: mystery
scene: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.
text: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}
> left: Sit in the moonlight
  mana +small
  mood -small
  say: {The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.|You sit with the moth tapping at the glass and the moon in the window. Nothing happens, very deeply, for an hour.}
> right: Sweep away your footprints
  guile +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.|You sweep your prints away and leave the others. By the next visit, yours are back, in the same places.}

@event seventh_tower_lake_window
title: The Window Over the Lake
place: seventh_tower
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: wave
tags: mystery danger
scene: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.
text: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == "winter": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}
> left: Open it and listen
  challenge research using arcana journeyman- wild:
    win:
      mana +small
      lore +tiny
      say: {You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.|You open it without deciding to. The lake is quiet, and then not, and then quiet again, as if it changed its mind about you.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.|The window opens on its own before your hand gets there. You shut it fast, and the latch is cold for a week.}
> right: Leave it latched
  mood -tiny
  health +tiny
  say: {You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.|You leave it latched and rest your hand on the latch a while. It feels like keeping a promise to someone.}
